      "source": [
        "# The new diabetes drug- Gluco-No-Mo!"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "l-FaI9IlmB6V"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Consider a new drug being tested for diabetes reversal. It's called _Gluco-No-Mo_. HbA1c (Hemoglobin A1c) measures the average blood sugar (glucose) over the past 2-3 months, showing the percentage of red blood cell's hemoglobin coated in sugar. A reduction in HbA1c is a sign improving outcome. Below are the outcomes measured as a reduction in HbA1c levels for the control and treatment groups."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aI-5Mpm1QyC7"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "np.random.seed(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "# HbA1c reduction (higher is better)\n",
        "control = np.array([0.3, 0.4, 0.2, 0.1, 0.5, 0.3, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1])\n",
        "treatment = np.array([0.8, 0.7, 0.6, 0.9, 0.5, 0.7, 0.6, 0.8, 0.9, 0.7])"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Y9jfGmBdn5p4"
      },
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "##Task 1\n",
        "Calculate the observed in means between the control and treatment groups."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tt1nEDh7oF3e"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "obs_control_mean = control.mean()\n",
        "obs_treatment_mean = treatment.mean()\n",
        "print(f\"observed control mean={obs_control_mean}, observed treatment mean={obs_treatment_mean}\")\n",
        "obs_diff_mean = obs_treatment_mean - obs_control_mean\n",
        "print(f\"Observed differences in mean={obs_diff_mean}\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "TEHF13twoEpS",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "304b25d9-793d-4e1f-c1a6-64bdb694d969"
      },
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "observed control mean=0.28, observed treatment mean=0.72\n",
            "Observed differences in mean=0.43999999999999995\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "How confident are you that the difference you are seeing is actually because of the drug is effective? Can you estimate the confidence interval around this difference in means?\n",
        "\n",
        "We can of course do it if we were allowed to repeat this experiment a large number of times. However, since we have limited resources and time, is there any other way to do this with a single sample dataset?"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HPuX4XdporlN"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Bootstrapping - _\"How variable is my estimator?\"_"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2zDkqQjQQDGX"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Bootstrapping estimates the sampling distribution of a statistic by repeatedly resampling with replacement from the observed data. It is typically used for estimating standard errors, constructing confidence intervals and assessing bias of an estimator. To understand how bootstrapping works in more detail, take a look at [this video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xz0x-8-cgaQ).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Assumptions in bootstrapping\n",
        "* The observed sample approximates the population\n",
        "* Resampling mimics repeated sampling from that population"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-clp87RIQI8s"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Let's try bootstrapping the observed dataset to create a sampling distribution"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AyltyjMbrFrW"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Step 1 - Decide on the number of times you wish to bootstrap and\n",
        "# create an array of that size to store the differences\n",
        "B = 10000\n",
        "diff_in_means = np.zeros(B)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 2 - Create a loop to generate bootstrap samples for both control and\n",
        "# treatment. Also record the mean difference for each iteration\n",
        "for i in range(B):\n",
        "  new_control_sample = np.random.choice(control, size=10, replace=True)\n",
        "  new_treatment_sample = np.random.choice(treatment, size=10, replace=True)\n",
        "  mean_diff = new_treatment_sample.mean() - new_control_sample.mean()\n",
        "  diff_in_means[i] = mean_diff\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 3 - Plot the histogram of the mean differences\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot 1: default bins\n",
        "plt.figure() #makes it a separate figure\n",
        "plt.hist(diff_in_means, bins=10)\n",
        "plt.xlabel('Difference in means of HbA1c')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Counts')\n",
        "plt.title('Histogram with 10 bins')\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot 2: custom bins (0 to 1, step 0.1)\n",
        "plt.figure()\n",
        "plt.hist(diff_in_means, bins=np.arange(0, 1, 0.1))\n",
        "plt.xlabel('Difference in means of HbA1c')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Counts')\n",
        "plt.title('Histogram with bins of width 0.1')\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 4 - Estimate the 97% confidence interval\n",
        "np.percentile(diff_in_means, [2.5, 97.5]) #length of confidence interval is roughly 0.22\n"
      ],
